Under the current **CAP/DA Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2** (effective **Sept. 1, 2025**), NC Medicaid covers certain **equipment, modification, and technology** items/services when they are identified through the CAP/DA assessment and service-planning process. I checked amendments for this policy, and the amendment lookup returned **no separate amendments**; the authoritative document returned was the current 9/1/2025 version. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 1) What home modifications are allowed under CAP/DA?

The policy says modifications may be made in:

- the beneficiary’s **primary private residence** if owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a **rented residence** if the modifications are **portable**; or
- a rented residence when the modification is allowed by the owner.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy lists examples of covered home modifications, including:

- ramps;
- grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair/walker access;
- emergency egress if medically necessary because of physical limitations of the responsible party;
- bathroom modifications, including toilet, shower/tub, sink fixtures, faucet controls, plumbing changes, and turnaround space;
- bedroom modifications for hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications, including cabinets, sinks, faucet controls, plumbing changes, and turnaround space;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- non-skid surfaces;
- lifts used primarily inside the home;
- door handle and door modifications;
- smart-home devices when the beneficiary lives alone and the need is identified in the assessment and person-centered service plan;
- certain air filtration systems if the policy’s medical criteria are met; and
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space damaged or removed during an approved modification.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy also includes related covered costs/services such as:

- technical assistance in device selection;
- training in device use by a qualified assistive technology professional;
- purchase, permits, inspections, taxes, and delivery charges;
- installation;
- assessment of the modification by the case manager and applicable inspectors to verify safety and that it meets the beneficiary’s needs; and
- repairs, so long as repair cost does not exceed replacement cost and the item is not covered by warranty.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 2) What are the key limits/exclusions?

The policy excludes items such as:

- items **not of direct medical or remedial benefit** to the beneficiary;
- items covered under the **Medicaid State Plan DME/orthotics/prosthetics/home health supplies**;
- items that are **recreational in nature**;
- items with **general utility to a non-disabled beneficiary**;
- warranties and unrelated maintenance contracts;
- equipment for swimming pools, hot tubs, spas, and saunas unless otherwise specifically approved/medically necessary under the plan;
- replacement of equipment that was not properly used, was lost, or purposely damaged; and
- some security/video monitoring items not approved in the service plan.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

So, in practice, a home modification request should be tied to the beneficiary’s disability-related needs, not duplicate State Plan coverage, and not be something with ordinary household utility.

### 3) What documentation must the Multidisciplinary Team provide?

The clearest documentation language in the CAP/DA policy is that the request must include:

- a **comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment** completed by the **case manager**, identifying the equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, **when applicable**;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested equipment/adaptation/modification;
- for some equipment/modification requests, the **estimated life of the equipment** and the **length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit** from it must be included in the request; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date the equipment/modification was provided and the cost, with related charges maintained in the CAP Business system.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 4) How this relates to the MDT specifically

The policy text I retrieved uses the phrase **“comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment”** but says it is **completed by the case manager**. It also separately requires a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** and, when applicable, a **physician’s order**. So the documentation package appears to be:

1. **MDT-based declaration of need assessment** documented by the **case manager**;
2. **professional recommendation** supporting why the modification is needed; and
3. **physician order**, if applicable.

I could verify that the policy requires this multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment, but the retrieved text does **not spell out in more detail exactly which MDT members must sign it** or whether every team member must separately document concurrence. On the text I reviewed, the policy attributes completion of the assessment to the **case manager**.

## Bottom line

For CAP/DA home modifications, the request must be for an approved disability-related modification in the beneficiary’s primary residence (or allowable rental setting), must not be recreational/general-utility/duplicative of State Plan coverage, and must be tied to assessed need. The documentation NC Medicaid requires includes:

- the **comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment** completed by the **case manager**;
- **physician’s order**, when applicable;
- **recommendation by an appropriate professional**;
- and supplier **invoice/cost documentation**;  
plus, in some requests, the **estimated life** of the equipment and expected duration of benefit.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

